 but no, it's your turn, and valid actions are \"Play T6\" or \"Play W5\", meaning you are to discard.\n\nSo you must have just drawn a tile, bringing your hand to 13 + 1 = 14, and now must discard one.\n\nBut with four melds already exposed, your hand should only have one tile before drawing.\n\nBefore drawing: 12 (melds) + 1 (hand) = 13  \nDraw: +1 \u2192 14  \nThen discard one \u2192 back to 13, with possibility of winning if the draw completed the hand.\n\nBut in this case, you now have two singles: W5 and T6 \u2014 meaning before draw, you held one of them, say W5, then drew T6.\n\nSo prior hand: [W5] + four melds.\n\nAfter drawing T6, now [W5, T6].\n\nTo have a winning hand, you must be able to form **four melds and a pair** after discarding.\n\nBut discarding either leaves the other as singleton \u2014 so no pair.\n\nTherefore, **you cannot win on this turn** \u2014 you are not in tenpai.\n\nThus, you must discard with the goal of improving toward a future win.\n\nBest strategy: improve shape toward a viable yaku.\n\nOptions:\n- Try to complete **Pure Straight** in W suit: need [W4,W5,W6] and [W7,W8,W9]\n- You already have [W1,W2,W3] and [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 but W4 is gone (0 left), so no one can use W4 anymore \u2014 so [W4,W5,W6] is impossible for anyone? No \u2014 the meld [W2,W3,W4] is already formed \u2014 so tiles are already used. W4 count is 0 \u2014 fully used up. So indeed, no more W4.\n\nBut you already have [W2,W3,W4] as a meld \u2014 so that\u2019s fine.\n\nFor Pure Straight, you need three non-overlapping chows: [W1,W2,W3], [W4,W5,W6], [W7,W8,W9]\n\nYou have first, but second requires W4 \u2014 which is gone, but you already used it in [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 but that\u2019s a different meld \u2014 overlapping.\n\nIn MCR, **Pure Straight** requires three chows of 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in the same suit, non-overlapping.\n\nBut [W1,W2,W3] and [W2,W3,W4] share tiles \u2014 so cannot count as both 1-2-3 and 4-5-6.\n\nMoreover, you don\u2019t have [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 you have [W2,W3,W4], which is 2-3-4.\n\nSo you have two chows: 1-2-3 and 2-3-4 \u2014 this is not helpful for Pure Straight.\n\nInstead, you might aim for **Quadruple Chow** \u2014 but that requires four identical chows.\n\nYou have two different W chows: [W1,W2,W3] and [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 not identical.\n\nAnd one T chow [T1,T2,T3], and one T pung [T9,T9,T9].\n\nNo quadruple chow possible.\n\nAlternatively, **All Chows** \u2014 worth 2 points. You already have four chows/pungs? [T9,T9,T9] is a pung \u2014 so not a chow.\n\nAll Chows requires **four chows and a pair of suited tiles**.\n\nYou have:\n- [T1,T2,T3]: chow\n- [W1,W2,W3]: chow\n- [W2,W3,W4]: chow\n- [T9,T9,T9]: pung \u2014 not a chow\n\nSo only three chows and one pung \u2014 not four chows.\n\nTherefore, not eligible for All Chows.\n\nTo qualify, you would need to eliminate the pung and have four chows.\n\nBut you can't undo a declared meld.\n\nSo your hand structure prevents All Chows.\n\nOther options: **Mixed Triple Chow** \u2014 three chows of same numbers in different suits.\n\nYou have [T1,T2,T3] and [W1,W2,W3] \u2014 that\u2019s two chows of 1-2-3 in different suits.\n\nIf you could get [B1,B2,B3], that would make it \u2014 but B1 has 2 unseen, B2 has 2, B3 has 1 \u2014 possible, but not in hand.\n\nAnd you have no such meld.\n\nSo no.\n\nPerhaps **Short Straight** \u2014 2 points for two consecutive chows in same suit, e.g., 2-3-4 and 5-6-7.\n\nYou have W: 1-2-3 and 2-3-4 \u2014 which overlaps \u2014 not successive.\n\nSuccessive would be 1-2-3 and 4-5-6, or 2-3-4 and 5-6-7.\n\nYou have 1-2-3 and 2-3-4 \u2014 this is overlapping, not successive.\n\nSo no Short Straight.\n\nGiven all this, your hand has limited potential.\n\nBest course: minimize risk and discard the safest tile.\n\nAs established:\n- W5: previous player completed [W4,W5,W6] via Chi \u2014 so they likely have W5 or are building around W5-W6-W7.\n- They may be waiting for W7, W5, or W6.\n- W5 is therefore dangerous \u2014 could feed their hand.\n- T6: no player has shown interest in T6. T5 is scarce (only 1 unseen), so [T4,T5,T6] or [T5,T6,T7] unlikely.\n- T6 is relatively safe.\n\nMoreover, you have no way to form a strong hand soon.\n\nAnother consideration: **One Voided Suit** \u2014 if you end up without one suit, you get 1 point.\n\nCurrently, you have W (Characters), T (Bamboos), and no Dots (B) in your melds? Wait \u2014 your melds: T and W \u2014 so two suits.\n\nYou have no B in your own melds \u2014 but you have B3, B1, B4, B6, B7, B8 in history \u2014 you yourself played B3, B1, B4, B3 \u2014 so you had B tiles, but no meld.\n\nYour current hand is W5 and T6 \u2014 so you still have access to both W and T.\n\nDiscarding T6 would leave only W5 \u2014 so your hand would be all W and T \u2014 still two suits.\n\nBut if you discard T6, you reduce T presence \u2014 but still have T1,T2,T3 and T9\u00d73.\n\nSo you will always have T and W.\n\nYou cannot achieve one voided suit \u2014 you have too many T tiles.\n\nSimilarly, you have no honors in hand \u2014 but you previously had F1,F2,F3,J1,J2,J3 \u2014 all discarded.\n\nSo likely **No Honors** is possible \u2014 1 point.\n\nBut not a major factor.\n\nFinal decision:\n- Keep W5: slight offensive potential; W5 could pair or form [W5,W6,W7] if you draw W6/W7.\n- Discard T6: safer, less likely to help opponents.\n\nPrevious player is strong in W \u2014 they have [W1,W2,W3], [W4,W5,W6], and possibly building upper W.\n\nThey discarded W1, W2, etc., but that doesn't mean they're not collecting \u2014 sometimes players discard to mislead, but in MCR, once a meld is exposed, it's fixed.\n\nTheir melds are [F3,F3,F3], [W1,W2,W3], [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so they have two W chows: 1-2-3 and 4-5-6.\n\nNext step: they may want [W7,W8,W9] for **Pure Straight** or **Full Flush**.\n\nSo they may be waiting on W7, W8, or W9.\n\nW7 has 3 unseen \u2014 still available.\n\nW5: they already have a full set [W4,W5,W6], so they don't need another W5 \u2014 unless for kong or pair.\n\nBut they haven't shown W5 elsewhere \u2014 so probably not holding extra.\n\nStill, safer to avoid W5 discard.\n\nT6: no evidence of need.\n\nAlso, T9 is down to 1 unseen \u2014 you have a pung of T9 \u2014 so someone might be waiting on T9 \u2014 but you're not discarding it.\n\nConclusion: **discard T6** is safer.\n\nReason: T6 is less likely to complete an opponent's hand compared to W5, which could assist the previous player who is heavily invested in the Character suit. Keeping W5 offers slightly better potential for developing a winning hand in the Characters suit.\n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
